From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DolphinSlicer <build@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 04:25:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] Use portable random distributions so seeded results match
 on all platforms

The C++ standard fixes the random engines but not the distribution algorithms or
std::shuffle, so libstdc++, libc++ and the MSVC STL turn the same seed into different
numbers. Code that seeds an engine for deterministic output therefore still differs
between Linux, macOS and Windows (and WebAssembly):
- monotonic fill (chain_monotonic_regions): libc++'s uniform_int_distribution(0, 0)
  consumes no draw and libstdc++'s does, so the following zig-zag orientation draw
  differs and the fill comes out mirrored;
- seam placement samples (TriangleSetSampling) and mesh decimation order
  (ShortEdgeCollapse) used std::uniform_real_distribution / std::shuffle;
- filament grouping used std::shuffle.

Add PortableRandom.hpp, which reproduces libstdc++'s algorithms exactly (verified
bit-for-bit against libstdc++ 15 for mt19937, mt19937_64 and minstd_rand), and use it
at those call sites. Linux results are unchanged; other platforms now match Linux.
Upstreamable: yes.
